[Music]  the pandemic has been a huge hit to our  economy and  thousands of businesses have lost  customers for others though there is a  silver lining  in canada the vast majority of  businesses are small and medium-sized  they employ most of the workers in the  private sector small and medium-sized  businesses contribute  55 percent of canada's gdp the pandemic  ground almost everything to a halt just  look at the number of jobs lost in early  2020 for example  well in may some signs of hope the  economy added 290  000 new jobs some business owners are  actually thriving  because of this pandemic and they can  barely keep up with demand  michael couture has the stories of some  of them  [Music]  they are the literal and figurative  signs of the times  everywhere you go these days you're  directed where to stand  how to move around a business by taped  arrows and decals  an interaction with sales staff is kind  of face to face  if you don't count that plexiglas  barrier the virus is unpredictable  so too are the spin-off industries that  are helping us  cope with covid19  10 weeks ago 12 weeks ago it was  pandemonium the action has leveled off  somewhat at this glass manufacturer in  ottawa  at the height of the pandemic the demand  for plexiglass was way beyond anything  tom zubkov  had seen after 40 years in the business  there has  never ever been anything like this where  all of a sudden  at the snap of fingers this had to  happen immediately there was no gradual  climbing of a hill there was no  production thoughts there was nothing  it just happened immediately and it had  to happen now or  so they started making three sizes that  could stand alone and be a barrier to  protect essential workers  business is still brisk it's just as  busy at miller mcconnell signs but that  wasn't the case at the beginning of the  pandemic when the ottawa marathon  and other major events were canceled so  too were the contracts to supply all  those signs  they had the acrylic in house so they  quickly shifted  to making face shields it was definitely  we wanted to help out the community  because we were in a position to  provide this much needed ppe but  a part of it was also trying to keep as  many people employed as possible  they're also donating hundreds to  community organizations and other  communities in the far north  weeks ago when provinces started to ease  restrictions  the core business bounced back the  changing times  meant a different type of sign is now  needed  you've probably already seen those floor  decals in nearly every store or business  you've been to lately  telling people where to stand and  helping them to keep a safe physical  distance  we've even pitched these kid-friendly  concepts to the city of ottawa  from municipal facilities as daycares  start opening up and camps and  and all that sort of stuff there's going  to be  fun colors with pictures of animals  numbers  you know go sit on this butterfly or  whatnot that kind of forward thinking is  helping some businesses boom  while others go bust during the covet 19  crisis  as the canadian economy reopens  businesses are reorienting it's believed  that will also help us get through the  recovery even as the threat of the  coronavirus remains  small businesses are guided largely by  what consumers want  and right now one of the chief things  that consumers want  is a safer experience when they're out  there doing their daily errands  and their important shopping  that could be why we're being bombarded  on social media by ads for gadgets  that'll keep us safe from covid19  from the phone sanitizer to the endless  variety of  anti-germ keys their effectiveness  remains unproven but personal protective  equipment  is in demand there is increasing  pressure to localize supply chains so  canada  isn't forced to rely on other countries  we have the technology  we have the ingenuity we have the  engineering might the manufacturing  might there's nothing  we can't build right here in ontario  enter alex dugal who's going all in  on medical masks not gonna lie it's  probably one of the scariest business  ideas  i've ever come up with scary because it  cost half a million dollars up front  he imported the mask making machine from  china so people in this country will  stop  buying masks from that country our hope  is is that canadians and governments and  businesses will support local and if our  our material and our finished product is  a comparable price to what they were  paying before  that they'll consider swapping over to a  canadian company to buy that product  his idea is to move beyond basic blue  into more colors and even customizable  for corporate logos  he's hoping to roll out these for canada  day  dugal also believes the uncertainty  around covid19  means masks will be a mainstay  eventually when everyone gets back to  going to big group events more than  likely they're going to need a face  covering  so our thought is is that we've got that  group of buyers but also at the end of  the day  healthcare needs these year-round  [Music]

